WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.290 --> 00:00:10.040
I am going to start
off this presentation

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:13.730
with every software engineer's
favorite thing to see,

00:00:13.730 --> 00:00:16.340
which is an open GitHub issue.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:18.800
So the issue that is
on the screen right now

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.500
is from the flexdashboard
GitHub page.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.920
And flexdashboard is widely regarded
as one of the easiest ways

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:28.890
to get a Shiny app
dashboard up and running.

00:00:28.890 --> 00:00:30.570
Here are the GitHub
users asking how

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.530
to make a pretty straightforward
app with a dashboard layout

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:37.620
that appears to be tackling
the seriously troubling issue

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:39.540
of cookie theft.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:40.690
So the layout is simple.

00:00:40.690 --> 00:00:43.440
There's two rows
with a top row having

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.800
a tabset on the left and a
terribly troubling plot

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.320
with an upward
trajectory on the right.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.980
The bottom is a large panel
that spans the entire width,

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.200
and it's terribly
going to be filled

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.630
with some very depressing data.

00:00:57.630 --> 00:00:59.220
But there are a few
interesting things

00:00:59.220 --> 00:01:02.030
about this screenshot that I
want to draw your attention to.

00:01:04.709 --> 00:01:06.480
OK is this better?

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:10.260
OK so one of the first things I
want to draw your attention to

00:01:10.260 --> 00:01:15.750
is that this issue was opened
in May of 2016, also known

00:01:15.750 --> 00:01:17.190
as the before times.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:20.700
So imagine the devastation
on cookie supplies

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:22.350
that have been wrought
in the six years

00:01:22.350 --> 00:01:23.940
that this GitHub
user has been waiting

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.370
to build this dashboard.

00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.850
So next is below, which is
the thumbs up emoji responses.

00:01:30.850 --> 00:01:34.770
And there are 18 of them, which
is by far the most up thumbed

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.570
issue on this repo.

00:01:36.570 --> 00:01:39.030
So clearly other users
have been equally troubled

00:01:39.030 --> 00:01:42.370
by burglary of a
confectionery nature.

00:01:42.370 --> 00:01:46.390
So why is this issue still open?

00:01:46.390 --> 00:01:49.150
If we scroll down,
you can actually

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:52.900
see this is JJ's response,
which is, you can't actually

00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:55.440
create this right now.

00:01:55.440 --> 00:01:57.330
So at this point, you
might be wondering

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:00.720
why I would dedicate the
first section of my talk

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:02.640
to pointing out the
shortcomings of software

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.140
written by the CEO of the
company that I worked for.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:10.500
And really, I
think it's worth it

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:13.080
because I think it perfectly
encapsulates and motivates

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:16.350
the work that I'm about to show.

00:02:16.350 --> 00:02:19.680
So if you went to
Joe's keynote, he

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:21.430
mentioned this in a similar way.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:24.840
But I really view
Shiny's superpower

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:27.600
as being this force
multiplier, as Joe puts it,

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:30.060
for data scientists to share
their work to a broader

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:31.140
audience.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:34.050
But really, there's
one kind of hiccup

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:35.670
in that one kind of
hurdle that we've

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:39.582
placed in front of data
scientists in using Shiny.

00:02:39.582 --> 00:02:41.290
And to understand what
that is, I kind of

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.180
want to start with
a question, which

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:46.360
is what is not crucial to
doing great data science.

00:02:46.360 --> 00:02:48.460
You know, there's a
lot of correct answers

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.370
to this question, but
the one I'm going for

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.730
is understanding the
tangled, terrible web

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:57.460
of getting the browser
to show what you want

00:02:57.460 --> 00:02:59.290
and where you want it.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:02.380
So Shiny does a pretty
decent job of this already.

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:05.830
We have helper functions,
but it could be better.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:09.260
Like why did this GitHub
issue have to be posted?

00:03:09.260 --> 00:03:12.190
Why did this user have trouble
building an app that they

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.620
could so clearly draw out?

00:03:14.620 --> 00:03:18.960
Shouldn't that be enough
to create your app?

00:03:18.960 --> 00:03:22.440
So more concretely, I want to
ask the question of how can we

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.010
make constructing the UI of
your Shiny app take less time

00:03:26.010 --> 00:03:28.880
and just generally
be more enjoyable?

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:30.800
So we want you,
the data scientist,

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.900
to be freed up to do
data science and not

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:34.760
fiddle with your UI code.

00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:37.370
And this has really been a
big focus of the Shiny team

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:38.570
for a while now.

00:03:38.570 --> 00:03:41.300
And I've been personally working
on it for more than a year

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:44.420
and have come up with two
fairly complementary paths

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.200
to try to address this question.

00:03:47.200 --> 00:03:49.090
So the first one
is how do we make

00:03:49.090 --> 00:03:53.560
declaring your app layout more
straightforward and intuitive?

00:03:53.560 --> 00:03:57.820
And the other one is, how do
we provide a new way of writing

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.870
the UI code for your app?

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:03.120
So I'm going to start with
the first question, which

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:05.745
is how do we make declaring the
layout of your app a little bit

00:04:05.745 --> 00:04:07.735
more intuitive and
straightforward?

00:04:07.735 --> 00:04:09.360
So the answer that
we're proposing here

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.010
is called grid layout.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.960
So what is grid layout?

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:18.740
First and foremost, grid
layout is an R package

00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:20.750
and it provides a
simple interface

00:04:20.750 --> 00:04:25.110
for laying out complex
data dashboards.

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:27.750
And also it's built
on a thing called

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:32.560
CSS grid, which is a spec, a CSS
spec built right into your browser.

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:35.010
So there's no external
libraries or JavaScript

00:04:35.010 --> 00:04:37.290
that needs to be run to work.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:39.808
And also because it's
built into your browser,

00:04:39.808 --> 00:04:41.100
it's not going to be abandoned.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:45.950
The project isn't going to
be left and not work later.

00:04:45.950 --> 00:04:48.390
So how do we actually go
about using gridlayout?

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.070
I'm going to give
you a quick example

00:04:50.070 --> 00:04:51.500
in code.

00:04:51.500 --> 00:04:55.580
And so we start by using a
gridlayout aware container.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.210
In this case, we're
using grid_page.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:00.770
We have other containers
for doing like nesting grids

00:05:00.770 --> 00:05:02.790
or putting them in
more traditional apps.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.400
But here we're doing
grid_page, which

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:08.240
leaves out your entire
app page with grid layout.

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:10.260
So after that, we
define the layout.

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:12.090
And here this is
the layout defined.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:14.750
It's a table, but the
table is represented

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.730
as a character
vector where there's

00:05:16.730 --> 00:05:18.470
two elements in each
element represent

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:22.040
the row of your layout, and
then each column of your layout

00:05:22.040 --> 00:05:27.720
is represented as basically
one or more spaces in this one

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.720
or more spaces rule allows
you to line things up

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:30.800
like you see here.

00:05:30.800 --> 00:05:34.790
So on the top, we have
two columns that say tabs.

00:05:34.790 --> 00:05:37.520
Next to that we have cookies
and on the bottom we just

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.040
have bottom all the way across.

00:05:41.040 --> 00:05:46.020
So then to place items in this
grid, you just use grid_card.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:48.750
The familiar syntax
kind of like tabsetPanel.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:51.450
So here like at the
top, we use grid_card

00:05:51.450 --> 00:05:53.790
and we place it in the
tabs area of the layout

00:05:53.790 --> 00:05:56.960
that we've just defined
using area = "tabs".

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.160
We just have to match
each one of those areas

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.660
to grid_card or its friends,
such as grid_card_plot.

00:06:03.660 --> 00:06:08.280
And this is the output of that
exact code that you just saw.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:12.630
And it looks pretty similar to
something we've already seen,

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.260
which is the layout that
the dashboard GitHub

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:18.150
issue is requesting.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.360
And so this kind of
feels pretty simple,

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:24.690
but really it covers most
of what gridlayout does.

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.130
And really gridlayout's
whole goal

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:28.260
is to be this very simple way.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:29.650
I can think of an app.

00:06:29.650 --> 00:06:33.060
I can draw it in a text table.

00:06:33.060 --> 00:06:34.270
I can make my app look like that.

00:06:37.460 --> 00:06:40.380
OK so that's the first way
that we've tackled this.

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.660
How do we make constructing
the UI tiny app more enjoyable?

00:06:44.660 --> 00:06:46.080
So let's look at the next way.

00:06:46.080 --> 00:06:48.950
And the next way is how can we
provide a new way of writing

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:51.650
the UI code for your app?

00:06:51.650 --> 00:06:54.620
And the solution that
we're proposing here

00:06:54.620 --> 00:06:58.520
called shinyuieditor.

00:06:58.520 --> 00:07:00.780
So the name may give
some of it away.

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:04.550
And also Joe's keynote yesterday
may have given some of it away.

00:07:04.550 --> 00:07:09.020
But let's quickly clarify
what the shinyuieditor is.

00:07:09.020 --> 00:07:12.950
The shinyuieditor is a
drag-and-drop visual editor

00:07:12.950 --> 00:07:14.817
for your Shiny app UI.

00:07:14.817 --> 00:07:16.400
So this GIF that
you're seeing kind of

00:07:16.400 --> 00:07:19.230
demonstrates that in
pretty concise form.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.560
You can drag elements
around, you can move them,

00:07:21.560 --> 00:07:25.750
you can resize them, and it
builds your UI doing that.

00:07:25.750 --> 00:07:28.250
And don't worry, we're going
to show a little bit more about

00:07:28.250 --> 00:07:30.110
in a few minutes.

00:07:30.110 --> 00:07:32.830
The shinyuieditor is
also an R package.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.413
It's not a very
interesting R package.

00:07:34.413 --> 00:07:36.830
There's really one function
that you're going to be using,

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:38.970
which is launch editor,
but that's how you get it.

00:07:38.970 --> 00:07:40.850
You go just like
any other R package

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.970
and store it that way
that you get the shinyuieditor.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:48.360
So more philosophically,
the shinyuieditor

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.230
is an attempt to help you
focus on the fun stuff.

00:07:52.230 --> 00:07:56.070
And if you've ever spent a
bunch of time doing things

00:07:56.070 --> 00:07:58.980
like tweaking a plot size
or making a column slightly

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:02.792
bigger by incrementing a
pixel value up and testing it,

00:08:02.792 --> 00:08:04.000
you kind of know what I mean.

00:08:04.000 --> 00:08:05.700
And the UI editor
is really an attempt

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:08.460
to make take that away, let
you just visually drag it

00:08:08.460 --> 00:08:11.130
to where you want it to be, and
then let the UI editor write

00:08:11.130 --> 00:08:13.810
the code for you.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:16.480
So now that we've established
kind of what the UI editor is,

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:18.580
let's talk about, I think
just as importantly,

00:08:18.580 --> 00:08:20.940
what the UI editor is not.

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:24.090
The UI editor is not
a new UI framework.

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:28.290
It provides like a visual way of
declaring the UI for your app,

00:08:28.290 --> 00:08:32.250
but it writes that in
whatever framework you want.

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.830
However, we hope that you
really like gridlayout

00:08:34.830 --> 00:08:38.280
right now because that's
the only supported layout.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:40.590
And this is actually kind
of a little historical quirk

00:08:40.590 --> 00:08:43.409
that when we started
this whole journey,

00:08:43.409 --> 00:08:45.030
we started with grid
layout and then I

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:48.150
made a little visual editor
for editing those tables,

00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:50.860
and then it was quickly
realized that there's more here.

00:08:50.860 --> 00:08:52.770
Let's expand this.

00:08:52.770 --> 00:08:54.870
But right now, you
use the UI editor,

00:08:54.870 --> 00:08:56.140
you're also using grid_layout.

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.030
But that's going
to change in the future.

00:08:59.030 --> 00:09:01.760
So kind of on the
same vein, it's

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:03.390
not going to lock
you into using it.

00:09:03.390 --> 00:09:07.550
So by virtue of it just
writing code for you,

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.190
if you don't want to use
it, you just stop using it

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:11.752
and you just start
editing your code

00:09:11.752 --> 00:09:13.710
like you would edit any
other Shiny app though.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:17.733
Maybe your app is too complex
for the visual editor and maybe

00:09:17.733 --> 00:09:20.150
you just, you know, are using
too many things that are not

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.160
supported in it right now.

00:09:22.160 --> 00:09:23.023
That's fine.

00:09:23.023 --> 00:09:25.190
You're not going to have
to go back to the beginning

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.180
and rewrite your app.

00:09:26.180 --> 00:09:28.880
And we really put
a fair amount of effort

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:31.550
into making sure that the
code the editor produces

00:09:31.550 --> 00:09:34.160
is human readable.

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:37.740
So this is something that has
been really important to us.

00:09:37.740 --> 00:09:41.100
And so in a similar
vein, shinyuieditor

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:45.030
is not ready for production,
but it writes code for you.

00:09:45.030 --> 00:09:48.060
So, like, in theory, if
that code worked and does

00:09:48.060 --> 00:09:49.990
what you want, go for it.

00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:52.060
But, you know, it's
still very early.

00:09:52.060 --> 00:09:54.480
It's in this alpha stage.

00:09:54.480 --> 00:09:56.710
We just haven't had
that many hands on it.

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:58.530
And so we really
are looking forward

00:09:58.530 --> 00:10:01.330
to having everybody
get their hands on it.

00:10:01.330 --> 00:10:05.340
File issues, you know,
find areas that you would.

00:10:05.340 --> 00:10:08.080
You find nice about it and
what you wish it could do.

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:11.250
But don't trust that you're
going to go home after this

00:10:11.250 --> 00:10:15.720
and immediately start using
it for your production app.

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:19.900
So shinyuieditor is also
not available for Python.

00:10:19.900 --> 00:10:25.050
So you may have noticed that
RStudio now Posit and the Shiny

00:10:25.050 --> 00:10:28.050
team have been making inroads
in the Python community,

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.510
but the UI editor has not.

00:10:30.510 --> 00:10:34.410
So this is just for
Shiny for R right now.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:37.830
That being said, as both the
UI editor in Shiny for Python

00:10:37.830 --> 00:10:42.910
mature, the goal is to
bring it for Python as well.

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:45.310
OK so now that we've kind
of established what it is

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:47.310
and what it isn't, let's
talk about the workflow

00:10:47.310 --> 00:10:49.170
of using the UI editor.

00:10:49.170 --> 00:10:51.420
So we start by launching the
editor with that function

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:53.580
that we mentioned before,
launch_editor you pointed

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:57.480
at your app location and
this application can exist

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.280
or it might not exist.

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:01.890
And if it doesn't exist,
we'll ask you a few questions

00:11:01.890 --> 00:11:06.870
and you can start with template
app that UI editor friendly.

00:11:06.870 --> 00:11:09.990
Once you've done that, the
UI editor spins up a browser

00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:13.470
window, you can use the visual
interface to edit your app code

00:11:13.470 --> 00:11:18.360
and then you close your browser
window and you enjoy the app.

00:11:18.360 --> 00:11:19.950
Optionally, you can
rinse and repeat,

00:11:19.950 --> 00:11:21.700
and I think that this
is really important.

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:23.430
We're not setting
this UI editor up

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:25.230
to be a way that
you start your app

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:27.880
and then you go on to
never using it again.

00:11:27.880 --> 00:11:32.200
It can ingest apps that it wrote
ingest app that in theory it didn't

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.910
write, and then so you can
have it kind of carry you

00:11:35.910 --> 00:11:39.400
through your whole Shiny
app development process.

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:41.410
Maybe later on you
need to add a new plot.

00:11:41.410 --> 00:11:43.470
You can do that
with the UI editor,

00:11:43.470 --> 00:11:47.900
not just the way of setting up.

00:11:47.900 --> 00:11:50.300
That's all good and
interesting to hear

00:11:50.300 --> 00:11:52.190
a bunch of tech
snippets and me read

00:11:52.190 --> 00:11:54.990
about what the UI editor is,
but it's the visual tool.

00:11:54.990 --> 00:11:57.740
I think it's probably
valuable to actually see

00:11:57.740 --> 00:11:59.890
what it looks like.

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:04.510
So I was going to do a live
demo, but I was told, Oh.

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:12.710
Well there we go.

00:12:12.710 --> 00:12:16.790
OK never say.

00:12:16.790 --> 00:12:18.120
So we're going to start here.

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:19.470
Let me talk quick.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:23.630
So we have a fresh
RStudio project open.

00:12:23.630 --> 00:12:26.010
There's nothing in it can
see in the files pane.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:26.760
It's pretty small.

00:12:26.760 --> 00:12:27.635
I apologize for that.

00:12:27.635 --> 00:12:30.410
But there's nothing
sitting in there.

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.320
And so we've run this
launch editor function

00:12:33.320 --> 00:12:35.210
and pointed it to the
current app directory

00:12:35.210 --> 00:12:37.280
and it says we
can't find an app.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:40.040
So we fill out
some template that

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.250
says, yeah, we want to create
a new app from a template.

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:45.520
And so we say, yes,
we do, and then it

00:12:45.520 --> 00:12:46.770
asks us what template we want.

00:12:46.770 --> 00:12:48.920
So we have a couple of
templates right now.

00:12:48.920 --> 00:12:49.890
Rewind this.

00:12:52.743 --> 00:12:53.660
A couple of templates.

00:12:53.660 --> 00:12:55.600
So there's geyser
templates and then

00:12:55.600 --> 00:12:57.050
there's this chick
weights template.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:13:01.030
And I don't know about you
guys, but my very first job

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.520
was actually as a
chicken farmer before I

00:13:03.520 --> 00:13:06.010
had this cushy
software developer job.

00:13:06.010 --> 00:13:08.175
And so I was not a park
Ranger at Yellowstone.

00:13:08.175 --> 00:13:09.800
So I'm not going to
use the geyser app.

00:13:09.800 --> 00:13:11.560
I'm going to do chick weights.

00:13:11.560 --> 00:13:14.210
I've seen enough geysers thing.

00:13:14.210 --> 00:13:15.710
So we choose that way.

00:13:15.710 --> 00:13:19.040
It spins up the UI editor
and this is what we have.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:20.790
And so this is in a browser tab.

00:13:20.790 --> 00:13:22.790
And so looking at
what we have here,

00:13:22.790 --> 00:13:25.990
we start we have this
element panel on the left.

00:13:25.990 --> 00:13:28.850
You can drag and drop elements
from that into your app.

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:31.440
Then in the middle, there's
this kind of app pane.

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:32.310
It's the skeleton.

00:13:32.310 --> 00:13:33.440
It's not exactly your app.

00:13:33.440 --> 00:13:36.410
We have faux plots
there, and on the right

00:13:36.410 --> 00:13:37.707
we have a properties panel.

00:13:37.707 --> 00:13:39.290
This properties panel
tells you things

00:13:39.290 --> 00:13:42.470
like where the element
is within your UI tree

00:13:42.470 --> 00:13:44.490
and then you also
have an app preview.

00:13:44.490 --> 00:13:48.290
So that App Main window
that you saw isn't perfect.

00:13:48.290 --> 00:13:52.780
So if you really need to
see what your app like.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:55.300
And so we have to plots
on top of each other here,

00:13:55.300 --> 00:13:58.160
and maybe we want to put those
plots next to each other.

00:13:58.160 --> 00:13:59.150
So we can do that.

00:13:59.150 --> 00:14:01.930
Just by reaching up and
adding a new column,

00:14:01.930 --> 00:14:04.490
we can drag one of the plots
over and drag one of the plots

00:14:04.490 --> 00:14:04.990
up.

00:14:04.990 --> 00:14:07.040
Now we have this whole
empty row on the bottom.

00:14:07.040 --> 00:14:08.643
We can just delete that.

00:14:08.643 --> 00:14:10.810
So we could check to make
sure that work and our app

00:14:10.810 --> 00:14:15.500
is updated instantly to
build that new layout.

00:14:15.500 --> 00:14:17.740
And so from there we
might say, you know,

00:14:17.740 --> 00:14:21.070
let's maybe let's shrink
this column on the left

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.740
and emphasize the
line plots on the right.

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:26.798
Do that and it automatically
updates.

00:14:26.798 --> 00:14:28.840
And so one of the things
we might want to do also

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:31.660
is right now, the
number of lines

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:35.200
that are shown in that plot
is given via numeric input.

00:14:35.200 --> 00:14:36.580
But maybe we want
to use a slider

00:14:36.580 --> 00:14:37.940
so we can delete that element.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:40.660
We can drag a slider input
in, go to the input ID,

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:43.240
change it to the numCh icks,
which is what

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:46.240
that reactive input is set as.

00:14:46.240 --> 00:14:48.770
And then we can go
and change the value.

00:14:48.770 --> 00:14:51.850
So we're going to
say Min 1, Max 20 and

00:14:51.850 --> 00:14:54.580
we're going to use step
size of 1.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:57.940
And then we can change the
label to say number of chicks.

00:14:57.940 --> 00:15:00.540
Then we can go back to the app
preview to see if that worked.

00:15:00.540 --> 00:15:02.130
And we can drag it and see yep,

00:15:02.130 --> 00:15:05.630
The app is actively
updated and it is working.

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:07.670
This kind of workflow
of going back and forth

00:15:07.670 --> 00:15:09.140
between the preview
and the editor

00:15:09.140 --> 00:15:11.577
is what we envisioned
people doing.

00:15:11.577 --> 00:15:13.410
The next, we might want
to change the title.

00:15:13.410 --> 00:15:15.000
So it's this Chick
Weights right now.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:19.250
Let's change it to
RStudio Conf 2022

00:15:19.250 --> 00:15:21.260
and we'll left align
it so we can

00:15:21.260 --> 00:15:24.360
see in the little tiny preview
on the bottom that it updated.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:26.660
But I want to emphasize
another point, which

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:29.960
is the fact that you can
edit the code as well.

00:15:29.960 --> 00:15:32.540
So we go into the app
code that the UI editor

00:15:32.540 --> 00:15:35.670
generated for us previously
there was nothing there.

00:15:35.670 --> 00:15:39.680
And what we see we can
find this RStudio Conf 2022

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:42.840
and let's change that
right in the code,

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:49.010
make it Posit::Conf and 2022
but make sure it's an integer

00:15:49.010 --> 00:15:52.960
so we'll add an L. Then we
save that and we go back and

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:55.020
the UI editor is
automatically updated.

00:15:55.020 --> 00:15:56.820
And so this is
really emphasizing

00:15:56.820 --> 00:15:59.670
that we have this workflow of
some things might be easier

00:15:59.670 --> 00:16:02.970
to do in the UI editor and
some things might be easier

00:16:02.970 --> 00:16:04.312
to do in code.

00:16:04.312 --> 00:16:05.770
And we don't want
to hold you back.

00:16:05.770 --> 00:16:07.437
We want the UI editor
to just sit there.

00:16:07.437 --> 00:16:10.380
Maybe you just want to use
it to resize columns or add

00:16:10.380 --> 00:16:13.650
a new plot, but you want to
type your contents or whatever

00:16:13.650 --> 00:16:14.460
by hand.

00:16:14.460 --> 00:16:15.960
There's no, you can
go back and forth

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:17.127
between the code and the UI.

00:16:17.127 --> 00:16:21.400
Edit or leave it up and just
edit your app as you go.

00:16:21.400 --> 00:16:24.620
And so we can just check
now to make sure that worked.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.650
So we can open up the
app that it generated,

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:27.880
the app that it generated.

00:16:27.880 --> 00:16:31.240
And so it worked... Good.

00:16:31.240 --> 00:16:33.490
Given that I pre-recorded
this demo, that would be

00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:35.940
pretty bad if it didn't work.

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:37.100
OK so.

00:16:40.400 --> 00:16:43.850
OK so quickly, I just want
to talk about the philosophy

00:16:43.850 --> 00:16:45.645
of the UI editor.

00:16:45.645 --> 00:16:47.770
So one thing that you might
be asking at this point

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:52.210
is how do we avoid some of
the common pitfalls of no code

00:16:52.210 --> 00:16:52.880
solution?

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:56.710
Because the UI editor is, in
a way, a no code solution.

00:16:56.710 --> 00:16:58.510
And this is something
that we were very

00:16:58.510 --> 00:17:00.970
careful about when we started.

00:17:00.970 --> 00:17:04.089
And I think the simplest
answer to that is

00:17:04.089 --> 00:17:06.339
that it's not a no code editor.

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:08.530
We're fundamentally
writing code.

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:10.990
The UI editor is a
visual interface,

00:17:10.990 --> 00:17:14.290
but everything goes immediately
back to code and vice versa.

00:17:14.290 --> 00:17:17.630
And so really, I don't
think of it as no code.

00:17:17.630 --> 00:17:20.752
I really think of it is
like a fancy autocomplete,

00:17:20.752 --> 00:17:22.960
you know, you might be able
to tab complete something

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:24.290
and it does what you want.

00:17:24.290 --> 00:17:26.279
This is essentially
doing that for your UI.

00:17:28.990 --> 00:17:31.810
And so we also really
tried to keep it as simple

00:17:31.810 --> 00:17:35.050
as we possibly could, because
one of the things that

00:17:35.050 --> 00:17:37.990
happens with no code
editors is that code

00:17:37.990 --> 00:17:42.680
is an extremely wonderful
medium for expressing yourself.

00:17:42.680 --> 00:17:46.210
RStudio is really a
proponent of the power of code.

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:49.780
And so the problem with
that extreme flexibility

00:17:49.780 --> 00:17:52.810
is that there's a
lot of solution space

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:54.760
that you need to make
available if you really

00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:56.620
want to fully explore that.

00:17:56.620 --> 00:17:59.660
And so visibly mapping to that
space can be really intense.

00:17:59.660 --> 00:18:02.680
So we really carefully pulled
everything back to make sure

00:18:02.680 --> 00:18:05.570
that we just let you
edit your UI code.

00:18:05.570 --> 00:18:08.270
You can move things, you
can set some settings.

00:18:08.270 --> 00:18:10.960
We're really not trying to go
crazy and let you do everything

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:14.050
because we found that in a
lot of tools that do that, you

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:16.930
actually might as well just
learn how to code instead

00:18:16.930 --> 00:18:21.100
of some esoteric visual
editor, because at least then

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.630
you know how to code.

00:18:22.630 --> 00:18:27.140
Don't have some skill that's
not going to work anywhere else.

00:18:27.140 --> 00:18:29.660
And so I think the last and
most important part about it

00:18:29.660 --> 00:18:34.950
is that this is the Shiny UI Editor
and that's an important fact.

00:18:34.950 --> 00:18:37.980
It's the UI Editor it's
not the Shiny app.

00:18:37.980 --> 00:18:40.710
We're not trying to
write your server code.

00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:42.520
We want you to write that.

00:18:42.520 --> 00:18:44.567
I think that you're the
best person to do that.

00:18:44.567 --> 00:18:46.150
We want you to, as
the data scientist,

00:18:46.150 --> 00:18:48.140
to write your cool plots
and your good models

00:18:48.140 --> 00:18:49.178
and all that stuff.

00:18:49.178 --> 00:18:51.470
We're just going to make it
a little bit easier for you

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:55.820
to build the UI if we don't want
you to have to be an expert web

00:18:55.820 --> 00:19:00.500
developer to create a nice
looking layout for your app.

00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:04.860
So the UI editor is the UI
editor, not the server editor.

00:19:04.860 --> 00:19:06.710
It's not a full
no code solution.

00:19:06.710 --> 00:19:09.980
That being said, there
are solutions out there

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:13.340
that are full no code that
write the server code for you.

00:19:13.340 --> 00:19:15.410
And there's actually
talk coming up after this

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:19.900
that I'm pretty excited
to see those just that.

